2 marks

Solve, algebraically.
\(\frac{5!(n-5)!}{3!(n-6)!}=80\)

\section*{Solution}
\(\frac{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3!(n-5)(n-6)!}{3!(n-6)!}=80\)
\[
\begin{aligned}
20(n-5) & =80 \\
n-5 & =4 \\
n & =9
\end{aligned}
\]

1 mark for factorial expansion ( \(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for each) \(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for simplification of factorials
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for solving for \(n\)

2 marks

Justify that 2.1 is a better estimate than 2.6 for the value of \(\log _{5} 29\).

\section*{Solution}
\[
5^{2}=25 \quad 5^{3}=125
\]
\(\log _{5} 25=2 \quad \log _{5} 125=3\)
29 is closer to 25 than 125
\(\therefore \log _{5} 29\) is closer to 2 than 3

1 mark

When \(p(x)=x^{3}+k x+6\) is divided by ( \(x+2\) ), the remainder is 4 . Determine the value of \(k\).

\section*{Solution}

\section*{Method 1}
\(4=(-2)^{3}+k(-2)+6\)
\(4=-8-2 k+6\)
\(4=-2-2 k\)
\(6=-2 k\)
\(k=-3\)
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for \(x=-2\)
1 mark for remainder theorem
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for solving for \(k\)

\section*{2 marks}

\section*{Method 2}
\[
x=-2
\]
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for \(x=-2\)

